FastCGI transport: buffered reads and accept4(SOCK_CLOEXEC) in main/fastcgi.c

Pure syscall reduction on the worker's request path, nothing changes on the wire:

 * safe_read() now reads whatever the peer has already sent (up to 16 KB) into a
   per-request buffer and serves record headers/bodies from it. A typical request
   head (BEGIN_REQUEST + PARAMS + empty PARAMS + empty STDIN) is one read() instead
   of six. Body chunks >= 16 KB with an empty buffer are read straight into the
   caller's buffer (no extra copy). Buffered data belongs to the connection and is
   kept across keep-alive requests; it is dropped when the fd is closed.
 * accept4(..., SOCK_CLOEXEC) replaces accept() + fcntl(F_GETFD) + fcntl(F_SETFD)
   when available. HAVE_ACCEPT4 comes from AC_CHECK_FUNCS([accept4]); today only
   ext/sockets/config.m4 checks it, so fpm-ng adds the check in sapi/fpmng/config.m4.
   An upstream submission should move the check into configure.ac. Without
   HAVE_ACCEPT4 the old accept()+fcntl path is compiled.

Measured on the fpm-ng test box (Linux, PTI+IBRS): -5 syscalls per keep-alive
request, -7 per fresh connection; numbers in docs/NOTES.md.

Czeka na: nie zgloszone jeszcze do upstreamu — kandydat na PR do php/php-src,
niezalezny od 0001 i 0002. Znika, gdy upstream przyjmie rownowaznik.
Sprawdzone na: PHP-8.3, PHP-8.4, PHP-8.5, master (patrz patches/README.md).

